Subject: [overlay 0039] dlight priority instead of first-come, and no stale dlight bits

Headset report, 1.0.11.16, rerelease data, base1, VR: "as I move my head, portions of walls
and floors switch between a lighter and a darker version; turning off dynamic lighting fixes
it." 0037 fixed the 64-bit mask defect; this is what was left. Diagnosis (read-only, file:line
verified): artifacts/vr-r14/DIAG-wall-lighting-r14.md. Two mechanisms, BOTH upstream defects
with nothing stereo about them — a flat desktop with the rerelease data has them too. VR only
makes them constant, because head POSITION moves every frame there and a mouse-look desk
player's does not.

M-A — src/client/view.c: MAX_DLIGHTS is a FIRST-COME cap.
MAX_SHADOW_LIGHTS is 256 (inc/shared/shared.h) and MAX_DLIGHTS is 64 (inc/refresh/refresh.h).
CL_AddShadowLights walks the shadowlight configstrings in configstring order calling
V_AddLightEx, whose first statement was `if (r_numdlights >= MAX_DLIGHTS) return;`. The
per-light distance fade a few lines below is a function of cl.refdef.vieworg and returns 0
past fade_end, at which point that light does not take a slot. Move the head a few
centimetres, a faded light crosses zero, frees its slot, and the next configstring light that
previously did not fit is admitted AT FULL INTENSITY. That is a step from nothing to
everything, in both directions, and it is exactly "the wall has a lighter and a darker
version". Fix: keep the 64 STRONGEST (intensity * lightstyle * fade) rather than the 64 that
came first — when the array is full, evict the weakest entry if the candidate beats it, else
drop the candidate. The light that loses is then always the one already contributing least,
so the step lands where it cannot be seen. Only slots V_AddLightEx itself owns are eligible
for eviction: V_AddLight's entries (muzzle flashes, rockets, explosions) put a radius in
dl->radius and a flat 1.0 in dl->intensity, so their weight is not comparable and a static
map light must never take a muzzle flash's slot. Cost: one float compare per light, a
64-entry rescan on the rare eviction, zero GPU cost.

M-B — src/refresh/tess.c: surf->dlightbits is consumed with no stamp check, and under
per-pixel lighting nothing ever clears it.
GL_DrawFace did `tess.dlight_bits |= surf->dlightbits;` unconditionally. Those bits are
indices into glr.fd.dlights[] for the CURRENT render only. world.c clears them only on a
re-mark (GL_MarkLights_r, and only for faces some light actually visits); GL_PushLights
returns before update_dynamic_lightmap for dlight reasons under ppl (upstream guard); and
overlay 0031 deliberately stopped clearing them in update_dynamic_lightmap under ppl, because
there they are the frame's shader light list rather than a lightmap companion. So a face lit
once — a blaster flash, a shadowlight walked past — carried those bits forever, while M-A and
the fade renumber the dlights array every frame: a stale bit ends up naming a completely
different light. The batch union then injects that light into the UBO for every face sharing
texture+state, and the union's contents change with the view.

Fix: honour surf->dlightframe, which GL_MarkLights_r already maintains beside the bits. Three
hunks are needed for that to be correct rather than merely quiet:

  * tess.c GL_DrawFace — the gate itself.
  * world.c GL_DrawBspModel — GL_TransformLights bumps glr.dlightframe once per inline
    bmodel, and R_RenderFrame runs GL_DrawEntities before GL_DrawAlphaFaces. World alpha
    faces carry the world's stamp but would be drawn after it had moved on, so they would
    fail the gate and silently lose all dynamic light. Save the world stamp, restore it after
    the model is flushed, and hand it to the bmodel's own deferred alpha faces as they are
    added (the bits index the same array either way; only the stamp differs).
  * surf.c update_dynamic_lightmap — 0031 left the bits alone under ppl but still zeroed the
    stamp. Now that the bits are gated on the stamp, that zero would drop the dynamic lights
    of any ppl face that merely had a lightstyle change this frame, which is the one route by
    which GL_PushLights still reaches this function under ppl. Stamp and bits are one value;
    under ppl neither belongs to this function.

Cost: one comparison per face, and likely a small win — fewer lights per batch means fewer
dlight UBO uploads.

Not done here, deliberately: widening the mask past 64 (uint64_t -> two words, BIT_ULL -> a
helper, touching gl.h/world.c/surf.c/mesh.c/tess.c/shader.c and doubling the dlight UBO). That
is the correct long-term fix for M-A and is a round of its own; the priority cap makes the
current 64 behave sanely first.

Composes with 0031 (repeat-frame lighting idempotence — this makes its ppl carve-out
consistent on both sides) and 0033 (eye-independent dlight cull — untouched).
Both fixes are upstreamable as-is.

Apply: patch -p1 -d vendor/q2repro < this

